Setting (from the previous page). X = U_{0,3}^{an} = P^1(C) - {0,1,∞}. The group S_3 acts on X by permuting the three punctures. The quotient map is f(z) = (27/4) z^2(z-1)^2/(z^2-z+1)^3, which is 1728/j(λ) in Legendre normalization. The orbifold quotient X/S_3 is the j-line with weights 2 at f=1 (orbit {2,-1,1/2}), 3 at f=∞ (orbit {-j,-j^2}), and the cusp at f=0. Its orbifold fundamental group is Π = S^+_{0,3} = π_1(U_{0,3}, S_3). On the previous page this is identified with Π'_{0,3} = C^{+'}_{1,1}, the oriented triangulated cartographic group. Its generators satisfy l'_∞ l'_1 l'_0 = 1 and l'_1^2 = l'_∞^3 = 1, so it is Z/2 * Z/3 ≅ PSL(2,Z), which Grothendieck writes SL(2,Z)'.

Step 1 (this page, oriented case). Let ε_0, σ_0, ρ be the lifts of the local monodromies, defined up to conjugacy. Grothendieck concludes that one can choose conjugates ε'_0 of ε_0 and σ'_0 of σ_0 with:
- relation (14): ρ σ'_0 ε'_0 = 1, together with ρ^3 = σ'_0^2 = 1;
- these relations give a presentation of Π, so ε'_0, σ'_0, ρ generate it.
This is the standard presentation PSL(2,Z) = <σ, ρ | σ^2 = ρ^3 = 1>, with the parabolic (cusp) element ε = (ρσ)^{-1}.

Step 2 (non-oriented extension). Enlarge S_3 to T_{0,3} = S_3 × Z/2, where the Z/2 is plausibly complex conjugation, which commutes with S_3 since the three points are rational. This gives the non-oriented group S_{0,3} = π_1(U_{0,3}, T_{0,3}). He maps it isomorphically to the non-oriented triangulated cartographic group T'_{1,1}, which he says is ≅ GL(2,Z). He then predicts three reflection generators τ'_0, τ'_1, τ'_∞, analogous to σ_0, σ_1, σ_∞ in the cartographic groups, with presentation (17):
- τ'_i^2 = 1 for each i;
- (τ'_0 τ'_∞)^2 = 1, corresponding to l'_1;
- (τ'_1 τ'_0)^3 = 1, corresponding to l'_∞.
This is the (2,3,∞) triangle reflection group, i.e. the extended modular group PGL(2,Z).

Step 3 (compatibility). He wants the products of pairs of reflections to recover the oriented generators, relation (18):
- τ'_∞ τ'_1 = ε'_0 (linked to l'_0);
- τ'_0 τ'_∞ = σ'_0 (linked to l'_1);
- τ'_1 τ'_0 = ρ (linked to l'_∞).
Then ρ σ'_0 ε'_0 = τ'_1τ'_0 · τ'_0τ'_∞ · τ'_∞τ'_1 = 1 automatically, so (14) becomes the rotation subgroup of the reflection presentation.

Purpose. To fix explicit, compatible generators for the orbifold fundamental groups of U_{0,3}/S_3, oriented and with complex conjugation. These are identified with the (1,1) Teichmüller / cartographic groups, matching the (0,3)/(0,4) and (1,1) building blocks of the Teichmüller tower. This feeds into the 'équation des lacets' (loop equation) program for the Galois action. The next page starts a general formalism for G-covers of signed orbifold surfaces and their quotients.
